          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Sizing and Circuit Choice</a>
          <a href="#inlet-and-cord" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Inlet Boxes and Cords</a>
          <a href="#standby-atc" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Automatic Switches in Detail</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A transfer switch connects a generator to your home’s wiring while physically isolating it from the utility grid. Without one, a portable generator can only reach whatever you can run an extension cord to — which excludes the furnace, the well pump and usually the sump pump.
        </p>

        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Transfer Switch Actually Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            A transfer switch does two jobs at once, and the second is the one that matters most.
          </p>

          <p>
            First, it gets generator power into your household circuits, including the hardwired ones that have no plug — furnaces, well pumps, sump pumps, hardwired lighting. Second, and critically, it makes it physically impossible for generator power to flow back out onto the utility lines while the utility supply is disconnected.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Why Backfeeding Kills People</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">It energises lines crews believe are dead.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Feeding a generator into a dryer receptacle with a double-ended cord — sometimes called a suicide cord — pushes power back through your meter and onto the distribution network. Utility crews working to restore your service can be electrocuted by it. It also bypasses every circuit protection in your panel and is illegal in most jurisdictions. A transfer switch exists to make this impossible.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            That interlocking is a mechanical or electrical guarantee rather than a matter of remembering to switch something off. A properly installed transfer switch cannot connect the generator and the utility to the same circuits at the same time, regardless of what order anybody operates things in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Types of Transfer Switch Compared
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Works With</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Operation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Relative Cost</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Manual transfer switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">You flip it after starting the generator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Interlock kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slide plate on the panel; you switch breakers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lowest</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Automatic transfer switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standby</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Senses the outage and switches unattended</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Universal / service-rated</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Either</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whole-panel switching rather than selected circuits</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Manual Transfer Switch</h3>

          <p>
            A separate sub-panel installed beside your main panel, wired to a chosen set of circuits — typically six to twelve. During an outage you start the generator, plug it into an inlet box, and flip the switches for the circuits you want powered. It is clear, labelled and hard to get wrong, which is its main advantage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Interlock Kit</h3>

          <p>
            A metal plate fitted to your existing panel that physically prevents the main breaker and a generator backfeed breaker being on simultaneously. You slide the plate, switch the main off and the generator breaker on, then manage individual circuits with their own breakers. Cheaper than a manual switch and gives access to every circuit in the panel rather than a fixed selection.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Automatic Transfer Switch</h3>

          <p>
            The standby generator solution. It monitors utility power continuously and, on failure, signals the generator to start and transfers the load across within seconds — with nobody home and nobody doing anything. This is the entire value proposition of a standby system, and it is not available with a portable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What a Transfer Switch Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Precise pricing varies considerably with region, panel condition and how much wiring is involved, but the relative structure holds everywhere.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Relative Cost</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Interlock kit, hardware</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A plate and hardware; must match your panel brand</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Interlock kit, installed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low to moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electrician time plus an inlet box and cable</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Manual transfer switch, installed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sub-panel, inlet box, circuit wiring, labelling</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Automatic transfer switch, installed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Part of a standby installation with gas and pad work</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Generator inlet box</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needed for portable setups; the outdoor connection point</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Permit and inspection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Required in most jurisdictions; budget for it</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Labour Dominates</h3>

          <p>
            As with most electrical work, the hardware is the smaller share. What you are paying for is an electrician’s time to work inside a live panel, run cable to an outdoor inlet, identify and move the circuits you want backed up, and label everything so it can be operated correctly in the dark by someone who is not an electrician.
          </p>

          <p>
            That last point is worth paying for properly. A transfer switch you cannot confidently operate during a night-time outage is not much use, and clear labelling of which circuits are on which switch is part of a good installation rather than a nicety.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What the Installation Actually Involves
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Choose the circuits you want backed up</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Furnace, refrigerator, freezer, sump or well pump, some lighting and outlets, and the internet. This decision drives the switch size.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Confirm generator capacity matches</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">There is no point backing up circuits your generator cannot carry. Size the generator and the switch together, not separately.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Select switch type and size</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Manual switches are sized by circuit count; interlock kits must match your specific panel brand and model.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Site the inlet box</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An outdoor, weatherproof inlet where you can reach it with the generator cord, positioned so the generator sits well away from doors and windows.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Electrician wires the switch</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Circuits are moved into the transfer switch or the interlock and backfeed breaker are fitted, and cable is run to the inlet.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">6</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Permit and inspection</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Required in most jurisdictions. This is not red tape to route around — it is what confirms the isolation actually works.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">7</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Label everything clearly</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Which switch feeds which circuit, and the operating sequence, written where you will find it during an outage.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">8</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Test the whole chain</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Start the generator, transfer, and confirm each backed-up circuit actually works. Then repeat annually.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">This Is Not DIY Work</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Panel work is genuinely dangerous and usually regulated.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Installing a transfer switch means working inside a live electrical panel and altering the connection between your house and the grid. In most jurisdictions it legally requires a licensed electrician and an inspection, and for good reason — a mistake here can kill a utility worker rather than just tripping a breaker. Hire it out.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Choosing Which Circuits to Back Up
          </h2>

          <p>
            With a manual transfer switch you are committing to a fixed set of circuits, so the choice matters. With an interlock you retain access to everything and manage it with the existing breakers, which is one of that option’s genuine advantages.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Furnace or boiler — hardwired, and the reason many people buy a switch at all.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Refrigerator and freezer — usually on a kitchen circuit shared with other outlets.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Sump pump or well pump — critical, and typically hardwired.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A lighting circuit or two, chosen for the rooms you will actually occupy.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The outlet where the internet router and phone chargers live.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Garage door opener, if the alternative is being unable to get a car out.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to Deliberately Leave Off</h3>

          <p>
            Electric ranges, dryers and water heaters draw far more than most portable generators can supply, and including them in a transfer switch invites someone to switch them on during an outage and stall the machine. Leaving them out is a form of protection rather than a limitation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Air conditioning is the judgement call. A window unit circuit is reasonable on a large portable; central air conditioning generally is not, because its start-up surge exceeds what portable machines deliver. If air conditioning matters to you, that is a standby generator conversation rather than a transfer switch one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inlet-and-cord" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Inlet Box and Cord Nobody Budgets For
          </h2>

          <p>
            A transfer switch is only half the connection. Power has to get from the generator, which is outside and well away from the house, to the switch inside — and that means an inlet box and a cord rated for the job.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Component</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Does</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Choosing It</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Power inlet box</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Weatherproof outdoor connection point</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rated for your generator’s output amperage</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Generator cord</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Carries power from generator to inlet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Matched to both plug types and the current draw</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cord length</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Distance from generator to inlet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Long enough to site the generator safely away</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Conduit and cable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Inlet back to the switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sized by the electrician for the load and run</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Site the Inlet With Exhaust in Mind</h3>

          <p>
            The inlet box position determines where the generator ends up standing, and that is a carbon monoxide decision as much as a convenience one. Put it where the generator will sit well away from doors, windows and vents, with room to direct the exhaust away from the house. An inlet fitted beside the back door for convenience encourages exactly the placement that kills people.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cord Length and Rating</h3>

          <p>
            Generator cords are heavy and expensive, and the temptation is to buy the shortest one that reaches. Buy the length that lets you site the generator properly instead. Undersized or overly long thin cords cause voltage drop, which makes motors work harder and can prevent large loads starting at all — a problem that looks like an undersized generator but is actually an undersized cord.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="standby-atc" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Automatic Transfer Switches in More Detail
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are going the standby route, the automatic transfer switch is not an accessory — it is the component that delivers the entire value of the system.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>It monitors utility voltage continuously and detects a genuine outage rather than a momentary dip.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>It signals the generator to start, waits for it to stabilise, then transfers the load, typically within seconds.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>It transfers back automatically once utility power returns and has been stable for a set period.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>It runs the generator’s scheduled self-test, usually weekly, so faults surface before you need it.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Service-rated versions can switch the entire panel rather than a selected subset of circuits.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whole-Panel Versus Selected Circuits</h3>

          <p>
            Automatic switches come in both forms. A whole-panel service-rated switch backs up everything, which requires a generator sized for the whole house. A selected-circuit automatic switch backs up a defined list, which allows a smaller and cheaper generator. Many installations use the latter combined with load shedding, where lower-priority circuits drop automatically when demand approaches the generator’s limit.
          </p>

          <p>
            That load-shedding capability is worth asking about specifically when getting quotes. It can make a mid-size standby unit serve a house that would otherwise need full capacity, and the saving on the generator often exceeds the cost of the load management module.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding Instead of Fitting a Switch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A double-ended cord from a generator into a dryer or wall receptacle pushes power back onto the utility lines where crews are working to restore your service. It bypasses every circuit protection in the panel and is illegal in most jurisdictions. There is no version of this that is acceptable, however briefly it is used.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Generator and Ignoring the Switch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without a transfer switch or interlock, a portable generator only reaches what an extension cord can reach — which excludes the furnace, well pump and usually the sump pump. If those are why you are buying, the switch is part of the purchase rather than an optional accessory.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing the Switch Without Sizing the Generator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                There is no point backing up circuits your generator cannot carry, and a switch with more circuits than the machine can supply just invites overload. Decide the load list first, size the generator to it, then specify the switch to match. The two decisions are one decision.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fitting an Interlock That Does Not Match the Panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Interlock kits are specific to panel brands and models, and a plate that nearly fits is not acceptable — the physical interlocking is the entire safety mechanism. Confirm the exact panel model before buying, and let the electrician specify it if there is any doubt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Permit and Inspection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The inspection is what confirms the isolation between generator and utility actually works. Skipping it saves a modest fee and leaves you with an unverified connection to the grid, potential insurance problems, and a system whose one critical safety function nobody has checked.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a transfer switch for a portable generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You need one for any hardwired load — a furnace, sump pump or well pump has no plug for an extension cord. A transfer switch or interlock kit is also the only safe way to power household circuits, because it physically prevents generator power flowing back onto the utility lines.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a generator transfer switch cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hardware costs are modest, and the labour dominates. An interlock kit is the cheapest route, a manual transfer switch with a sub-panel is moderate, and an automatic transfer switch as part of a standby installation is the most expensive. Add an outdoor inlet box and a permit fee in most jurisdictions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a transfer switch myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most jurisdictions no, and it is not advisable in any case. Installation means working inside a live panel and altering the connection between your home and the grid, and a mistake can electrocute a utility worker. It legally requires a licensed electrician and an inspection in most places.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a manual and automatic transfer switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A manual switch requires you to start the generator and flip it yourself, and pairs with a portable. An automatic switch senses the outage, starts the generator and transfers the load within seconds with nobody present, and is part of a standby installation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many circuits should a transfer switch cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically six to twelve for a residential manual switch. Prioritise the furnace, refrigerator and freezer, a sump or well pump, one or two lighting circuits and the outlet feeding your internet. Leave off electric ranges, dryers and water heaters, which exceed most portable generators.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a permit for a transfer switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most jurisdictions yes, and the inspection is worth having rather than avoiding. It verifies that the isolation between generator and utility supply actually works, which is the one safety function the whole device exists to provide.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

