        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A transfer switch is what turns a generator from a way to keep the fridge cold into genuine household backup. It gets power to the hardwired loads that matter most — furnace, well pump, sump pump — and it physically prevents your generator energising the utility lines that crews are working on.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For a portable, the choice is between a manual transfer switch and an interlock kit. The manual switch is clearer to operate and covers a fixed set of labelled circuits; the interlock is cheaper and gives access to every circuit in the panel. Either is a proper solution, and both need an electrician.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you choose, decide the switch and the generator together rather than separately, get the permit and inspection, insist on clear labelling you can follow in the dark, and test the whole chain once a year. The one thing with no acceptable version is backfeeding through a receptacle — that is how utility workers get killed.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
